  <h2 id="why">Why biotechnology careers salary figures online feel so confusing</h2>

  <p>
    Search "biotechnology salary" and you will find one site claiming Rs 3 LPA, another claiming Rs 24 lakhs average, and a third somewhere in between.
  </p>

  <p>
    None of them are lying. They are measuring different things.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--amber">
    <p class="callout__eyebrow">The usual bad advice</p>
    <ul class="callout__list">
      <li>"Biotech pays badly," based on one BSc lab-assistant offer from a small company.</li>
      <li>"Biotech pays great," based on one senior CRO director's LinkedIn post.</li>
      <li>"A PhD is the only way up," ignoring regulatory affairs, bioinformatics, and QA leadership routes that do not require one.</li>
      <li>"Government science jobs pay nothing," ignoring DA, HRA, and pension-linked value on top of the basic pay-matrix figure.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="callout__footer">
      None of this tells you what your actual first offer will look like, or what would realistically move that number up.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    A biotechnology salary depends on three things above all else: the exact function you work in, whether your employer is government, private industry, academia, or a CRO/CDMO, and your degree level. Call this <strong>The 3-Lever Pay Framework</strong> - the same three levers explain almost every pay jump in this guide, so keep them in mind as you read.
  </p>

